     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p9-finding-k-theory",
     "replacement": "Exponential growth and decay problems usually involve finding the value of $k$.\n\nIf $\\frac{dQ}{dt}=kQ$, then $Q=Ae^{kt}$, where $A$ is the initial quantity.\n\n1. Write $Q=Ae^{kt}$ and substitute the initial quantity for $A$.\n2. Substitute the given values and solve for $k$. Leave $k$ in exact form.\n3. Rewrite the equation with $A$ and $k$.\n4. Solve for what the question asks.\n\nWhen you need to differentiate or solve for $t$, keep $k$ as a symbol instead of its exact value. This avoids writing a complex logarithm on every line.",
